            0.116,
            0.424,
            0.779,
            0.442
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Q5 Find out how many supercells are possible for different numbers of cells.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.451,
            0.881,
            0.487
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Do you notice any pattern? What is the method to fill a given table to get the maximum number of supercells? Explore and share your strategy.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.496,
            0.879,
            0.53
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ans. For even number of cells say,2,4,6,… the number of supercells would be respectively, 2/2 =1,4/2 =2,6/2=3,…"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.532,
            0.88,
            0.566
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For odd number of cells , say 1,3,5,7,… the number of supercells would be respectively (1+1)/2= 1, (3+1)/2 = 2, (5+1)/2= 3,(7+1)/2 = 4,…"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.567,
            0.881,
            0.603
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "To get the maximum number ofsupercells, we have to start by filling the first cell as super cell & then fill alternately."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.612,
            0.881,
            0.646
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Q6. Can you fill a supercell table without repeating numbers such that there are no supercells? Why or why not?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.655,
            0.882,
            0.69
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ans. No; the cell which is filled by the greatest number among the given numbers chosen, will become super cell irrespective of its position in the table."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.699,
            0.882,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Q7. Will the cell having the largest number in a table always be a supercell? Can the cell having the smallest number in a table be a supercell? Why or why not?",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.741,
            0.672,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ans. Yes, the largest number in a table will always be a supercell."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.769,
            0.881,
            0.805
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "No, the smallest number in a table can never be a supercell as the number in all the adjacent cells will be greater than it."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.115,
            0.813,
            0.85,
            0.831
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Q8. Fill a table such that the cell having the second largest number is not a supercell.",
